---
/**
 * Stage-curtain reveal, shown once per browser session on the first page the
 * visitor lands on.
 *
 * Two deep-navy panels sit closed over the viewport, the wordmark fades up
 * between them, then the panels part left and right like a stage curtain and
 * the layer takes itself out of the page.
 *
 * HOW IT IS SAFE
 *   - The parting is a pure CSS animation with `both` fill, so it plays and
 *     finishes even if no JavaScript ever runs. There is no way for the
 *     curtain to get stuck closed over the site.
 *   - prefers-reduced-motion and <html data-motion="off"> hide it outright
 *     (display:none, see src/styles/motion.css) rather than freezing it shut.
 *   - A one-line inline script in src/layouts/Base.astro's <head> adds
 *     html.curtain-seen after the first load of a session, which hides it for
 *     the rest of that session. If sessionStorage is blocked the curtain just
 *     plays again — no breakage.
 *
 * TO REMOVE IT: delete the <CurtainReveal /> line and the curtain <script>
 * block in src/layouts/Base.astro. The CSS can stay or go with it.
 *
 * Timing and colours live in src/styles/motion.css under "Curtain reveal".
 */
---
<div class="curtain" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="curtain-panel curtain-panel-l"></div>
  <div class="curtain-panel curtain-panel-r"></div>
  <span class="curtain-mark">UNICORE</span>
</div>

<script>
  // A view transition swaps in the next document's <html>, which drops the
  // class the head script set — so the curtain markup on the new page would
  // play again on every internal link. Put the class back before paint.
  document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', () => {
    document.documentElement.classList.add('curtain-seen');
  });
</script>
